namespace ExceptionCode
{
class HelloException : Exception
{
public HelloException() { }
public HelloException(string message) : base(message) { }
public HelloException(string message, Exception inner) : base(message,
inner) { }
}
class Program
{
static void Main(string[] args)
{
try
{
throw new HelloException("Hello is an exception!");
}
catch (HelloException ex)
{
Console.WriteLine("Exception Message:");
Console.WriteLine(ex.Message);
}
Console.ReadKey();
}
}
}
The output is as follows:
So, we can see from the preceding example that you just have to create a class that will
extend the Exception class. This class should have three constructors: one shouldn't take
any parameter, one should take a string and pass it to the base, and one should take a string
and an exception and pass it to the base.
Using a custom exception is like using any other built-in exception provided by .NET
Framework.
